{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read your reactive inputs such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" through a getter (for example backing them with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":") when creating the table, so the table sees updates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-ordering-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-ordering-alpine-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering (Alpine) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-affects-column-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What Affects Column Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./column-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Pinning"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A manually specified column order is applied."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions.groupedColumnMode"},{"type":"text","value":" is set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'reorder' | 'remove'"},{"type":"text","value":", then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-order-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Order State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you don't provide a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state to specify the order of the columns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"default-column-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default Column Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" table option to also specify the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", not both."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"managing-column-order-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managing Column Order State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state just like any other table state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without going through the component that owns the table. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/store"},{"type":"text","value":" is already a dependency of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/alpine-table"},{"type":"text","value":", so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAtom"},{"type":"text","value":" is available."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = createAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\n// subscribe wherever it is needed\ncolumnOrderAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to column order changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnOrderChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported by owning the slice in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":". It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({\n  columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'] as ColumnOrderState,\n})\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get columnOrder() {\n      return local.columnOrder // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: (updater) => {\n    local.columnOrder =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.columnOrder) : updater\n  },\n  //...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reordering-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reordering Columns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":". For example, with native browser drag events on the header cells. Keep the drag state in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":" so the markup can react to it:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const local = Alpine.reactive({ movingColumnId: null as string | null })\n\n// move the dragged column in front of the column it was dropped on\nfunction handleDrop(targetColumnId: string) {\n  const fromId = local.movingColumnId\n  if (!fromId || fromId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      newColumnOrder.splice(newColumnOrder.indexOf(fromId), 1)[0]!,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  local.movingColumnId = null\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" works the same whether the table manages the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state internally, you control it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnOrderChange"},{"type":"text","value":", or you own it with an external atom. The official "},{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" calls it with a full array of leaf column ids."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-ordering-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" to update the column order state directly. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset the order to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":", or pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to clear the order state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Columns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied."}]},{"type":"code","value":"column.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"drag-and-drop-column-reordering-suggestions-alpine","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Alpine)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. Here are a few suggestions:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consider native browser drag events ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@dragstart"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@dragenter"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@dragend"},{"type":"text","value":") with your own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.reactive"},{"type":"text","value":" state if you want zero dependencies. This can be very lightweight, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Material React Table"}]},{"type":"text","value":" implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies; the approach translates directly to Alpine since it is just DOM events feeding "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you want a library, look at framework-agnostic options such as Atlassian's "},{"type":"link","href":"https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pragmatic drag and drop"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Check maintenance status, bundle size, and how well they handle semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table>"},{"type":"text","value":" markup before committing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do NOT reach for React-only DnD libraries (including DnD Kit's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@dnd-kit/*"},{"type":"text","value":" packages). They depend on React's component model and do not work with Alpine."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"column-ordering-setup","text":"Column Ordering Setup","level":3},{"id":"column-ordering-alpine-guide","text":"Column Ordering (Alpine) Guide","level":2},{"id":"what-affects-column-order","text":"What Affects Column Order","level":3},{"id":"column-order-state","text":"Column Order State","level":3},{"id":"default-column-order","text":"Default Column Order","level":4},{"id":"managing-column-order-state","text":"Managing Column Order State","level":4},{"id":"reordering-columns","text":"Reordering Columns","level":3},{"id":"column-ordering-apis","text":"Column Ordering APIs","level":3},{"id":"drag-and-drop-column-reordering-suggestions-alpine","text":"Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Alpine)","level":4}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Column Ordering](../examples/column-ordering)\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Column Ordering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Column Ordering (Alpine) Guide\n\nBy default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the `columns` array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the `columnOrder` state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order.\n\n### What Affects Column Order\n\nThere are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual **Column Ordering** - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. [Grouping](./grouping) - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnOrder` state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning.\n\n### Column Order State\n\nIf you don't provide a `columnOrder` state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the `columns` array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the `columnOrder` state to specify the order of the columns.\n\n#### Default Column Order\n\nIf all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the `columnOrder` state in the `initialState` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are using the `state` table option to also specify the `columnOrder` state, the `initialState` will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either `initialState` or `state`, not both.\n\n#### Managing Column Order State\n\nIf you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the `columnOrder` state just like any other table state.\n\nIn v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without going through the component that owns the table. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = createAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\n// subscribe wherever it is needed\ncolumnOrderAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to column order changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnOrder` plus `onColumnOrderChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({\n  columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'] as ColumnOrderState,\n})\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get columnOrder() {\n      return local.columnOrder // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: (updater) => {\n    local.columnOrder =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.columnOrder) : updater\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Reordering Columns\n\nIf the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to `table.setColumnOrder`. For example, with native browser drag events on the header cells. Keep the drag state in `Alpine.reactive` so the markup can react to it:\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ movingColumnId: null as string | null })\n\n// move the dragged column in front of the column it was dropped on\nfunction handleDrop(targetColumnId: string) {\n  const fromId = local.movingColumnId\n  if (!fromId || fromId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      newColumnOrder.splice(newColumnOrder.indexOf(fromId), 1)[0]!,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  local.movingColumnId = null\n}\n```\n\n`table.setColumnOrder` works the same whether the table manages the `columnOrder` state internally, you control it with `state` + `onColumnOrderChange`, or you own it with an external atom. The official [Column Ordering example](../examples/column-ordering) calls it with a full array of leaf column ids.\n\n### Column Ordering APIs\n\nUse `table.setColumnOrder` to update the column order state directly. Use `table.resetColumnOrder` to reset the order to `initialState.columnOrder`, or pass `true` to clear the order state.\n\n```ts\ntable.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)\n```\n\nColumns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied.\n\n```ts\ncolumn.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()\n```\n\nThese helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order.\n\n#### Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Alpine)\n\nTanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. Here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. Consider native browser drag events (`@dragstart`, `@dragenter`, `@dragend`) with your own `Alpine.reactive` state if you want zero dependencies. This can be very lightweight, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. [Material React Table](https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering) implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies; the approach translates directly to Alpine since it is just DOM events feeding `table.setColumnOrder`.\n\n2. If you want a library, look at framework-agnostic options such as Atlassian's [Pragmatic drag and drop](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about). Check maintenance status, bundle size, and how well they handle semantic `<table>` markup before committing.\n\n3. Do NOT reach for React-only DnD libraries (including DnD Kit's `@dnd-kit/*` packages). They depend on React's component model and do not work with Alpine.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Column Ordering (Alpine) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Alpine examples:\n\n- [Column Ordering](../examples/column-ordering)\n\nRead your reactive inputs such as `data` through a getter (for example backing them with `Alpine.reactive`) when creating the table, so the table sees updates.\n\n### Column Ordering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  get data() {\n    return local.data\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Column Ordering (Alpine) Guide\n\nBy default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the `columns` array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the `columnOrder` state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order.\n\n### What Affects Column Order\n\nThere are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual **Column Ordering** - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. [Grouping](./grouping) - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnOrder` state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning.\n\n### Column Order State\n\nIf you don't provide a `columnOrder` state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the `columns` array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the `columnOrder` state to specify the order of the columns.\n\n#### Default Column Order\n\nIf all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the `columnOrder` state in the `initialState` table option.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are using the `state` table option to also specify the `columnOrder` state, the `initialState` will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either `initialState` or `state`, not both.\n\n#### Managing Column Order State\n\nIf you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the `columnOrder` state just like any other table state.\n\nIn v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without going through the component that owns the table. `@tanstack/store` is already a dependency of `@tanstack/alpine-table`, so `createAtom` is available.\n\n```ts\nimport { createAtom } from '@tanstack/store'\nimport {\n  createTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = createAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\n// subscribe wherever it is needed\ncolumnOrderAtom.subscribe(() => {\n  // react to column order changes\n})\n\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnOrder` plus `onColumnOrderChange` pattern is still supported by owning the slice in `Alpine.reactive`. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({\n  columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'] as ColumnOrderState,\n})\n//...\nconst table = createTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    get columnOrder() {\n      return local.columnOrder // connect the reactive slice back down to the table\n    },\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: (updater) => {\n    local.columnOrder =\n      typeof updater === 'function' ? updater(local.columnOrder) : updater\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Reordering Columns\n\nIf the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to `table.setColumnOrder`. For example, with native browser drag events on the header cells. Keep the drag state in `Alpine.reactive` so the markup can react to it:\n\n```ts\nconst local = Alpine.reactive({ movingColumnId: null as string | null })\n\n// move the dragged column in front of the column it was dropped on\nfunction handleDrop(targetColumnId: string) {\n  const fromId = local.movingColumnId\n  if (!fromId || fromId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      newColumnOrder.splice(newColumnOrder.indexOf(fromId), 1)[0]!,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  local.movingColumnId = null\n}\n```\n\n`table.setColumnOrder` works the same whether the table manages the `columnOrder` state internally, you control it with `state` + `onColumnOrderChange`, or you own it with an external atom. The official [Column Ordering example](../examples/column-ordering) calls it with a full array of leaf column ids.\n\n### Column Ordering APIs\n\nUse `table.setColumnOrder` to update the column order state directly. Use `table.resetColumnOrder` to reset the order to `initialState.columnOrder`, or pass `true` to clear the order state.\n\n```ts\ntable.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)\n```\n\nColumns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied.\n\n```ts\ncolumn.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()\n```\n\nThese helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order.\n\n#### Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Alpine)\n\nTanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. Here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. Consider native browser drag events (`@dragstart`, `@dragenter`, `@dragend`) with your own `Alpine.reactive` state if you want zero dependencies. This can be very lightweight, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. [Material React Table](https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering) implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies; the approach translates directly to Alpine since it is just DOM events feeding `table.setColumnOrder`.\n\n2. If you want a library, look at framework-agnostic options such as Atlassian's [Pragmatic drag and drop](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about). Check maintenance status, bundle size, and how well they handle semantic `<table>` markup before committing.\n\n3. Do NOT reach for React-only DnD libraries (including DnD Kit's `@dnd-kit/*` packages). They depend on React's component model and do not work with Alpine.\n"}
